Parse the minutes of a %z time zone offset exactly in strptime().
The code came from an old glibc that kept the offset in hundredths of an
hour: it converted the minutes with mm*50/30, so an offset was truncated
to a multiple of 36 seconds, and it refused offsets above 12 hours. So
date -d '2026-10-19 12:00 +0001' was 36 seconds off, and +1300 and
+1400, which are in use, were invalid dates.

Compute the offset in seconds directly, and drop the 12 hour limit, as
current glibc does.

---
 win32/strptime.c |   12 +++---------
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/win32/strptime.c
+++ b/win32/strptime.c
@@ -463,17 +463,11 @@
             else if (n != 4)
               /* Only two or four digits recognized.  */
               return NULL;
-            else
-              {
-                /* We have to convert the minutes into decimal.  */
-                if (val % 100 >= 60)
-                  return NULL;
-                val = (val / 100) * 100 + ((val % 100) * 50) / 30;
-              }
-            if (val > 1200)
+            else if (val % 100 >= 60)
+              /* Minutes valid range is 0 through 59.  */
               return NULL;
             if (gmtoff) {
-              *gmtoff = (val * 3600) / 100;
+              *gmtoff = (val / 100) * 3600 + (val % 100) * 60;
               if (neg)
                 *gmtoff = -*gmtoff;
             }
